title: Task status and progress
tags: Tasks
kind: reference
summary: pending, running, then done, failed or cancelled; a phase with optional counts, and the phases of a reconcile.

```
pending → running → done
                  → failed
                  → cancelled
```

* `pending` — registered, waiting for the repository (a query queued behind a reconcile). Already observable.
* `running` — working; its progress is updated as it advances.
* `done`, `failed`, `cancelled` — terminal, kept for a few seconds so a client polling just after still reads the outcome ([[Task registry and retention]]). `cancelled` is a stop you asked for, distinct from `failed` so that it does not read as an error.

!! Progress

The same shape for every kind:

```
{"phase": "fingerprint", "done": 1240, "total": 5000}
```

`phase` is a short, kind-specific label; `done` and `total` may each be `null` when
the amount of work is unknown. A client shows a bar when both are known and
`total > 0`, and a spinner labelled with the phase otherwise.

The duplicate scan's phases are in [[The duplicate scan]]; its `full` phase is the
only one of any kind that counts ''bytes'', and a client formatting counts should
pick the unit from the phase name.

!! The phases of a reconcile

|!Phase |!Counts |
|`walk (depth N, …)` |directories processed at the current depth |
|`stat` |the discovered paths, each stat'ed |
|`index` |the discovered paths, each resolved |
|`scan` |the tracked metarecords (orphan detection) |
|`fingerprint` |the orphaned metarecords |
|`similarity` |no counts; only with a similarity threshold |
|`create` |the new files |
|`refresh` |the discovered paths (when enabled) |
|`mime` |the files on disk (when enabled) |
|`metadata` |the files on disk: embedded metadata extraction (when enabled) |

The walk is a pure traversal — reading directories and their entries' types, no
`stat` — that collects every eligible path: cheap, with no knowable total, so it
reports progress by depth. Once it is done the path list is known, so every later
phase, starting with the expensive `stat` pass, has an exact total and a
determinate bar.

!! The phases of a query

A query's task reads `querying` until its work starts, then the phase the evaluator
is in — reported from inside it (`crate::progress`), so a long query shows where its
time goes while it runs:

|!Phase |!Counts |
|`preparing` |no counts: resolving paths and the forest's leaves ([[How queries are evaluated]]) |
|`filtering` |no counts: the bitmap work between the scans |
|`scanning <field>` |the candidates a text predicate checks one by one: those its literals leave in the text index, or the running intersection it probes ([[Query planner]]) |
|`comparing <field>` |likewise, for a `!=` or an ordered comparison probing the running intersection |
|`paging` |no counts: sorting, walking, fetching the page |
|`assembling` |no counts: reading the selected rows |

A scan walking a field's distinct values instead reports its phase without counts:
the number of keys it will read is not known beforehand. A counted phase reports
`0/total` on entry and every 256 candidates; a nested phase ending hands the phase
back to the one it ran in. A finished query keeps the last phase it entered.
